<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jurchens</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Jurchen</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Jurchens were a Tungusic people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a>. In the twelfth century they founded the Jin dynasty, which conquered northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty</a>. Their descendants, unified by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nurhaci">Nurhaci</a> and renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-taiji">Hong Taiji</a>, founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jurchens.jpg" alt="Jurchens" width="268" height="410" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jurchen_woodblock_print.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Khitan Liao state absorbed most of its territory and employed former Balhae officials, refugees strengthened the new Korean kingdom of Goryeo, and the <b>Jurchens</b>, who later founded the Jin dynasty, descended from Mohe peoples of the region.</blockquote><a href="/empire/balhae">Balhae · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Foreign trade passed through the port of Byeongnando near Kaegyong, visited by Song Chinese, Japanese, Khitan and <b>Jurchen</b> traders and, in the eleventh century, by Arab merchants, through whom the name Goryeo probably travelled west to become Korea.</blockquote><a href="/empire/goryeo">Goryeo · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Jin dynasty was founded by the <b>Jurchen</b>, a Tungusic people of the forests and river valleys of Manchuria who lived by farming, hunting, fishing and stock-raising and paid tribute to the Khitan Liao dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/jin-dynasty-1115-1234">Jin dynasty (1115-1234) · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…in Sejong's reign, the retired king Taejong sent an expedition against Tsushima to suppress Japanese pirates, and campaigns against the <b>Jurchen</b> in the 1430s and 1440s established the four commanderies and six garrisons that fixed the northern border on the Yalu and Tumen rivers, where it still lies.</blockquote><a href="/empire/joseon">Joseon · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…such as the Xi and the tribes of Mongolia, herded horses, sheep and cattle and hunted, and the <b>Jurchen</b> of Manchuria fished, farmed and traded furs, while in the south the Sixteen Prefectures and the Liao valley held a large population of Chinese farmers, along with Balhae settlers…</blockquote><a href="/empire/liao-dynasty">Liao dynasty · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Nurhaci's Banner system organised the entire <b>Jurchen</b> population into colour-coded units that were at once army formations, census registers and social structures.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qing-empire">Qing Empire · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
